.TH HSH 1 "August 2026" "hsh 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple Unix shell implementation in C
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple Unix command interpreter written in C. It reads commands from
standard input, separates each command from its arguments, and executes
built-in or external commands.
.PP
The shell supports both interactive and non-interactive execution modes.
.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
When standard input is connected to a terminal,
.B hsh
displays the prompt:
.PP
.RS
.B #cisfun$
.RE
.PP
The shell then waits for the user to enter a command.
.SH NON-INTERACTIVE MODE
When standard input is not connected to a terminal,
.B hsh
reads commands from standard input without displaying the prompt.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
.B echo "ls" | ./hsh
.B cat commands.txt | ./hsh
.B ./hsh < input.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
.TP
.B exit
Exit the shell using the currently stored command status.
.TP
.B env
Display each environment entry on a seperate line.
.SH COMMAND PARSING
The command line is split into a command and its arguments using spaces and
tab characters as delimiters.
.PP
Empty input lines are ignored. Leading and trailing whitespace is automatically
trimmed.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
Before executing an external command,
.B hsh
resolves the executable path.
.PP
If a command begins with
.B /
or
.B .
, the shell treats it as a direct executable path and checks whether it can
be executed using
.BR access (2).
.PP
If only a command name is provided, the shell searches for the executable in
the directories listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable.
.PP
When a valid executable is found, the shell:
.PP
.RS
1. Creates a child process using
.BR fork (2)
.RE
.RS
2. The child executes the command using
.BR execve (2)
.RE
.RS
3. The parent waits for the child using
.BR waitpid (2)
.RE
.RS
4. Stores the resulting exit status for future use
.RE
.SH PATH RESOLUTION
Commands given as direct paths (starting with
.B /
or
.B . )
are checked directly for executability.
.PP
Commands given without a direct path are searched for in the directories
contained in the
.B PATH
environment variable, in order from left to right.
.PP
If
.B PATH
is not available, or if no executable matching the command can be found, the
command is not executed and an error is reported.
.SH ERRORS
If a command cannot be found,
.B hsh
prints an error in the following form:
.PP
.RS
.B ./hsh: line_number: command: not found
.RE
.PP
Error status codes:
.TP
.B 127
Command not found
.TP
.B 1
Fork failure or execution error
.TP
.B N
Exit status of the last successfully executed command
.PP
If
.B fork
fails, the shell reports an error using
.BR perror (3)
and sets the status to 1.
.PP
If
.B execve
fails in the child process, the child reports the error and exits with
status 127.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hsh
stores the status of the last executed command.
.PP
When a child process terminates normally, the parent retrieves its exit
status with
.BR waitpid (2)
using
.BR WIFEXITED (3)
and
.BR WEXITSTATUS (3).
.PP
The
.B exit
built-in exits the shell using the currently stored status.
.PP
When end-of-file (EOF) is reached on standard input, the shell also exits
using the stored status.
.SH COMPILATION
Compile the project with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B gcc -Wall -Werror -Wextra -pedantic -std=gnu89 *.c -o hsh
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the shell in interactive mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B ./hsh
#cisfun$ /bin/ls
#cisfun$ env
#cisfun$ exit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Execute a command in non-interactive mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B echo "ls" | ./hsh
.B cat test.txt | ./hsh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Execute a command using an absolute path:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B /bin/echo hello
.fi
.RE
.PP
Display the environment:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B env
.fi
.RE
.PP
Exit the shell:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B exit
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B main.c
Program entry point. Contains the
.B main()
function.
.TP
.B shell.c
Contains built-in handling, external command execution, command parsing,
and the main shell control flow.
.TP
.B shell_loop.c
Contains prompt, input, command-processing, and child-process helper functions.
.TP
.B path_utils.c
Contains direct-path validation and PATH command resolution functions.
.TP
.B utils.c
Contains utility functions (trim, get_path_env, build_full_path).
.TP
.B shell.h
Header file with shared declarations, includes, and function prototypes.
.TP
.B README.md
Project documentation with flowchart and usage examples.
.TP
.B AUTHORS
List of project contributors.
.SH BUGS
This is a simplified shell implementation for educational purposes. It does not
support:
.PP
.RS
- Pipes (|)
.RE
.RS
- I/O redirections (>, <, >>, 2>, etc.)
.RE
.RS
- Background processes (&)
.RE
.RS
- Command substitution $(...) or `...`
.RE
.RS
- Wildcards/globbing (*, ?, [])
.RE
.RS
- Shell variables and parameter expansion
.RE
.RS
- Job control (fg, bg, jobs)
.RE
